Java 11, released in September 2018, is a Long-Term Support (LTS) version and brought several new features, API enhancements, and performance improvements. Below are the key Java 11 features explained with simple examples:

✅ 1. var in Lambda Parameters
Java 11 allows using var in lambda parameter declarations.

🔹 Example:

List<String> names = List.of("John", "Jane");
names.forEach((var name) -> System.out.println(name.toUpperCase()));
Useful for adding annotations to parameters in lambdas.

✅ 2. New String Methods
Java 11 added several utility methods to the String class:

🔹 isBlank() – Checks if the string is empty or contains only white space:

System.out.println("   ".isBlank()); // true
🔹 lines() – Converts a multi-line string into a stream of lines:

String multiline = "Hello\nWorld";
multiline.lines().forEach(System.out::println);
🔹 strip(), stripLeading(), stripTrailing() – Unicode-aware trim:

System.out.println("  Hello  ".strip());        // "Hello"
System.out.println("  Hello  ".stripLeading()); // "Hello  "
System.out.println("  Hello  ".stripTrailing());// "  Hello"
🔹 repeat(int count) – Repeats the string:

System.out.println("Hi ".repeat(3)); // Hi Hi Hi 
✅ 3. Files.readString() and writeString() (java.nio.file)
Simplifies reading/writing small text files.

🔹 Example:

Path path = Path.of("example.txt");
Files.writeString(path, "Hello Java 11");

String content = Files.readString(path);
System.out.println(content); // Hello Java 11
✅ 4. HttpClient API (Standardized)
Modern HTTP client introduced in Java 9, finalized in Java 11.

🔹 Example (Synchronous Request):

HttpClient client = HttpClient.newHttpClient();
HttpRequest request = HttpRequest.newBuilder()
    .uri(URI.create("https://example.com"))
    .build();

HttpResponse<String> response = client.send(request, HttpResponse.BodyHandlers.ofString());
System.out.println(response.body());
🔹 Asynchronous Request:

client.sendAsync(request, HttpResponse.BodyHandlers.ofString())
      .thenApply(HttpResponse::body)
      .thenAccept(System.out::println);
✅ 5. Local-Variable Syntax for Lambda Parameters
Allows annotations or use of var in lambda expressions.


list.forEach((@Nonnull var item) -> System.out.println(item));
✅ 6. Nest-Based Access Control
Improves access between nested classes and outer class members, even private ones, without reflection.

No syntax change, but better encapsulation and access control at bytecode level.

✅ 7. Collection.toArray(IntFunction)
New default method in Collection to convert collection to array without needing new Type[size].

🔹 Example:

List<String> list = List.of("a", "b", "c");
String[] array = list.toArray(String[]::new);
System.out.println(Arrays.toString(array)); // [a, b, c]
✅ 8. Running Java Files with java (No Compilation Needed)
Java 11 lets you run .java files directly like a script.

🔹 Command Line:
bash

java Hello.java
🔹 Example Hello.java:

public class Hello {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Hello from Java 11!");
    }
}
✅ 9. Removed/Deprecated Modules
Java 11 removed several deprecated Java EE modules:

java.xml.ws, java.xml.bind, java.activation

java.corba, java.transaction, java.se.ee

You’ll need to use external libraries if you relied on these.

✅ 10. ZGC: Low Latency Garbage Collector (Experimental)
Z Garbage Collector (ZGC) is a scalable low-latency GC, useful for large heaps.

Use with JVM flags: -XX:+UseZGC
(Still experimental in Java 11)

